  steps:[
    {t:"Belt assumptions", d:"These are the standard assumptions for belt problems, usually stated or assumed:\n• No slip between belt and pulley — any part of the belt in contact with the pulley has the same motion as the pulley surface, so v = rω and a = rα.\n• The belt does not stretch or break and stays taut — so every point on the belt has the same speed and the same magnitude of tangential acceleration."},
    {t:"a) Acceleration of point C", d:"a_C = a_B = rα, where r = 200/2 = 100 mm at B\na_C = (0.1)(−30) = −3 m/s²"},
    {t:"b) Angular velocity after 2 s", d:"ω₀ = 1000 rpm = 1000(2π)/60 = 104.7 rad/s\nω = ω₀ + αt = 104.7 − 30t\nω₂ = 104.7 − 30(2) = 44.7 rad/s"},
    {t:"c) Velocity of point C", d:"v_C = ωr = 44.7 × 0.1 = 4.47 m/s\n(Circular motion is used because the belt wraps onto the pulley without slipping at B, so it must have the same motion at B.)"},
    {t:"d) Number of turns in the first 2 s", d:"θ = ω₀t + ½αt² = 104.7(2) + ½(−30)(2²) = 209.4 − 60 = 149.4 rad\nTurns = 149.4 / (2π) = 23.8 revolutions\n(Get used to doing this by integration too — same method as T1.1 and T1.2.)"},
    {t:"e) When the pulley stops", d:"The pulley is not turning when ω = 0:\n104.7 − 30t = 0\nt = 3.49 s"}
  ],
  original:`As a "look forward", we need to make some assumptions here, which are usually specified (or assumed) for belt problems. Firstly, there is no slip between pulley and belt. This mean that any part of the belt contacting the pulley must have the same motion as the part of the pulley it contacts, ie v = rω, a = rα etc. Next, the belt doesn't stretch or break, and stays taut. This means that all points on the belt must stay the same distance apart along the belt's trajectory, so all points on the belt have the same speed (not velocity, which has direction), and the same magnitude of tangential acceleration (ie acceleration which speeds up or slows down the belt).

a)  a_C = a_B = rα  where r = 200/2 = 100 mm at B, and α = −30 rad/s²

        ∴ a_C = (0.1)(−30) = −3 m/s²

b)  Use rotational motion equation:   ω = ω₀ + αt
    (can get this by integrating the constant value of α, remembering that ω(0) is 1000 RPM)

        ω₀ = 1000 rpm = 1000(2π)/60 = 104.7 rad/s

        ω = ω₀ + αt = 104.7 − 30t

        ∴ ω₂ = 104.7 − 30(2) = 44.7 rad/s

c)  Velocity at point C:   v_C = ωr

        ∴ v_C = 4.47 m/s

    (use circular motion because the belt is wrapping onto the pulley without slipping at B,
     so must have the same motion at B)

d)  Use rotational motion equation:   θ = ω₀t + ½αt²

        (The linear equation for reference:  s = ut + ½at²)

        ∴ θ = ω₀t + ½αt² = 104.7(2) + ½(−30)(2²) = 149.4 rad

    (Get used to doing this via integration too — same method as Q1 and Q2.)

    To convert to revolutions: ÷ 2π:

        Number of turns = 149.4/(2π) = 24 revolutions

e)  The pulley is not turning when ω = 0

        ∴ ω = ω₀ + αt = 104.7 − 30t = 0

        ∴ t = 3.49 s`
